What this study changed in the hi_pr 3.5 distribution, and nothing else.

../../setup_libraries.sh fetches the archive (code/hipr-3.5.tar, md5
cb7bd4cf8db1f15b12e3a9228b6f774b), unpacks it to code/hipr-3.5/ and applies
parser.c.patch beside this file.  Unpack the archive elsewhere and diff to see
that the list below is complete.

1. parser.c, line 91, one character:

       -        pr_type[3],             /* for reading type of the problem */
       +        pr_type[4],             /* for reading type of the problem */

   The problem line is read with

       sscanf ( in_line, "%*c %3s %ld %ld", pr_type, &n, &m )

   and "%3s" writes three characters AND a terminating zero, so it needs
   four bytes and was given three.  The zero lands in whatever follows
   pr_type on the stack, and then strcmp(pr_type, "max") compares past the
   end of the array and usually fails.  Built with its own makefile flags
   (gcc -O4) by gcc 11.4 on this machine, stock hi_pr 3.5 rejects its own
   sample.inp with "line 1 of input - it is not a Max Flow problem line."
   It presumably worked in 2004 because the compiler of the day happened to
   put a zero byte there.  This is a bug in reading the input file; it is
   outside the measured region and touches no part of the algorithm.

hi_pr.c IS NOT CHANGED.  Every line of the method -- init, globalUpdate,
gap, relabel, discharge, stageOne, stageTwo, and the constants ALPHA, BETA
and GLOB_UPDT_FREQ -- is compiled from Goldberg's bytes.  ../solve_hipr.c
includes hi_pr.c with its main renamed by the preprocessor and supplies its
own shell around it; see the comment at the head of that file for what the
shell does differently and why.
